    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d205">

                <pb facs="0259" n="243" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg243"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.3527/4–2247: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Messersmith</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos Aires</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-04-22T18:00:00-03:00">April 22, 1947—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>454. For Merchant<anchor n="47" xml:id="d205-f1"/> from Landis. What policy have
                    you evolved regarding Sixth Freedom?<anchor n="48" xml:id="d205-f2"/> Argentines
                    worried by Peruvian potential acquisition of route to Buenos Aires, putting Peru
                    into Argentina–New York traffic. Same problem presented by Brazil, which,
                    incidentally, now does Sixth Freedom traffic as witness operations Panair Buenos
                    Aires to Paris. We have potentially same problem Montreal–Bermuda, as agreement
                    today specifically grants it, but would you be willing to object to it by, for
                    example, supplementary exchange of notes. [Landis.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Messersmith</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d205-f1" n="47" xml:id="d205-f1-content">
                <p>Livingston T. Merchant, Chief of the Aviation Division.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d205-f2" n="48" xml:id="d205-f2-content">
                <p>See telegram 338, April 24, 4 p.m., to Buenos Aires, <ref target="#pg245">p.
                        245</ref>.</p>
